example.paragraph:
Let $A$ be an invertible $n \times n$ matrix—say, $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \mathbf{a}_{1} & \cdots & \mathbf{a}_{n}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Then the columns of $A$ form a basis for $\text{ℝ}^{n}$ because they are linearly independent and they span $\text{ℝ}^{n}$, by the Invertible Matrix Theorem.

Let $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      0 \\
      {- 6}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 4} \\
      1 \\
      7
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ and $\mathbf{v}_{3} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      1 \\
      5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$ Determine if $\{\mathbf{v}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{v}_{2}\text{,~}\mathbf{v}_{3}\}$ is a basis for ${\mathbb{R}}^{3}$.

Since there are exactly three vectors here in $\text{ℝ}^{3}$, we can use any of several methods to determine if the matrix $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \mathbf{v}_{1} & \mathbf{v}_{2} & \mathbf{v}_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is invertible. For instance, two row replacements reveal that $A$ has three pivot positions. Thus $A$ is invertible. As in Example 3, the columns of $A$ form a basis for $\text{ℝ}^{3}\mspace{-4mu}\text{.}$

content.paragraph:
What about a matrix $A$ that is not in reduced echelon form? Recall that any linear dependence relationship among the columns of $A$ can be expressed in the form $A\mathbf{x} = 0$, where $\mathbf{x}$ is a column of weights. (If some columns are not involved in a particular dependence relation, then their weights are zero.) When $A$ is row reduced to a matrix $B$, the columns of $B$ are often totally different from the columns of $A$. However, the equations $A\mathbf{x} = 0$ and $B\mathbf{x} = 0$ have exactly the same set of solutions. If $A = \text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{a}_{n}
      \end{matrix}\text{]}$ and $B = \text{[}\begin{matrix}
      \textbf{b}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{b}_{n}
      \end{matrix}\text{]}$, then the vector equations

statement.paragraph:
Let $\mathbf{v}_{1} = {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 3} \\
      7
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,},$ $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      9 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{v}_{3} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      7 \\
      11 \\
      6
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,$ and $H\, =$ $\text{Span}\,\{\mathbf{v}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{v}_{2}\text{,~}\mathbf{v}_{3}\}$. It can be verified that $4\mathbf{v}_{1} + 5\mathbf{v}_{2} - 3\mathbf{v}_{3} = 0$. Use this information to find a basis for $H$. There is more than one answer.
